WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:05.530
- In August 2001,

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:09.700
the press descended on the town of
Bury St Edmunds in south-east England.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.050
The mayor was going to unveil

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:14.660
the world's first Internet Bench
here in the Abbey Gardens.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.100
I'm not sitting on the bench
for a couple of reasons.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.510
One, I can't get close-ups of
it while I'm sitting on it,

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.450
and two, it looks like quite a lot
of the local bird life

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.940
has been perched above it recently.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.360
Anyway, "internet bench" sounds ridiculous,

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.270
but you have to remember in
2001, there were no smartphones.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:32.650
Wifi did technically exist,
but it wasn't widespread.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.150
And mobile internet was limited
to a few specially coded,

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:36.480
tiny black and white sites,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.650
which could be accessed
slowly and expensively

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.140
on just a few models of phone.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.460
If you wanted an internet
connection in 2001,

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.110
your computer had to
be plugged in somehow,

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.354
you couldn't just expect to
check your email in a park,

00:00:47.354 --> 00:00:50.020
and thus: the Internet Bench.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.900
A bench with sockets you could
plug your computer's modem into,

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.678
and dial up to the
internet over a phone line.

00:00:55.678 --> 00:00:56.880
Which is weird, right?

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.650
'Cause surely the sensible
thing to do, even back then,

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.470
would've been to have
one internet connection,

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:01.910
maybe an ADSL line,

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.910
and then share it like a
regular corporate network,

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:06.820
just plug in an ethernet cable
and you'd have a connection.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.400
But no, you had to use a phone cable,
and your laptop would have to dial

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.520
a regular landline phone number
to connect to... well. Who?

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.660
Launch day photos make it very clear
who you were supposed to connect to,

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.205
or at least it would,
if the mayor had realised

00:01:19.205 --> 00:01:22.120
which way up stickers
need to be on a laptop.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.040
This was all a publicity stunt for MSN,
the Microsoft Network.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.200
And there are some very nice
quotes from MSN staff

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.380
in the BBC article about it,

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.670
which were probably pasted
straight from the press release.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.090
In truth, this wasn't
really an internet bench,

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:36.970
it was a bench with a couple
of phone extensions run to it,

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:37.803
and that was it.

00:01:37.803 --> 00:01:39.490
For a three-month trial period,

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.947
you could dial up to your own
internet account from here,

00:01:41.947 --> 00:01:45.010
and it didn't take long before some
local tech-savvy teenagers worked out

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:48.060
there wasn't any block on the numbers
you could call from here.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.300
The bench just had a
few phone sockets

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.380
wired into the exchange
at Microsoft's expense.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.860
So, of course, they made a couple of
international calls for free

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.700
and tried to get through to Bill Gates.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.700
They didn't manage it,

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.140
but they did get a nice
write-up in the local paper.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:06.450
And one of the most wonderful
early-2000s posed photos I have ever seen.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.300
From a technical standpoint,
this bench was a disaster,

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:10.930
but MSN were probably happy with it.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.570
They'd have been happy with
the teenage hackers, as well.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.930
It got their company
name in the paper again.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.360
They're probably still
happy with this video,

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.300
even as I tell you it was a terrible
half-assed idea. Because what it did,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.610
and what it's still doing,

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.450
was get their name and logo
in front of an audience.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.170
They just want to be noticed.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.170
That's one of the dirty secrets
of public relations.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.110
If you want to get the
attention of the press,

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.780
if you want your brand
in front of the world,

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.540
you don't have to actually
make something good,

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.370
or useful, or sensible, or that even
works like you said it would.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:40.410
The truth is malleable.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.000
You just have to tell
a story that resonates,

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.410
get the press in to take some pictures,

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:47.140
and figure they don't know enough about
the subject to ask technical questions.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.000
And then after you've
got those column inches,

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.860
or these days, the retweets and clicks,

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:54.120
you can basically abandon
the whole project.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.560
The public are fine with it.
It's a silly story that doesn't matter.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:57.773
The press are fine with it.

00:02:57.773 --> 00:03:00.170
They've got content and
sold the adverts around it,

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:03.000
and it doesn't matter if
it gets ripped apart later,

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.950
as long as it doesn't kill or maim anyone,
then you got your free advertising.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:08.590
And we've seen this
time after time after time.

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.790
Heck, I've done this
occasionally in the past.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.400
That's not even the same bench, actually.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.233
It's the right location,

00:03:14.233 --> 00:03:15.770
but it's been swapped
out for a different one

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:17.640
with a couple of plaques on it
and no phone sockets.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.160
But then, the bench working
was never the point.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.380
This wasn't about connecting
the town of Bury St Edmunds

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.630
or blazing a trail into the future,

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.750
it was about getting cheap advertising.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:29.690
And here I am, 20 years later,
still giving them that,

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:33.723
because World's First Internet Bench
still gets attention.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:39.753
Oh, someone sat in it.

